module lib/hf/guess
  Where the SCF starts. The SCF is a fixed-point iteration, so it needs a
  starting density, and when a molecule has more than one SCF solution the
  start decides which one it reaches. The guess therefore has members of its
  own, picked by `opts[OPT_GUESS]` (`gs_of`): the spin-averaged SCF
  (production), the core Hamiltonian, the generalized Wolfsberg-Helmholz
  matrix, zero, and a density the caller imports.

  Every member returns two spin densities, `[2, n, n]`, each built from
  orbitals occupied once; a closed-shell driver adds them.

gs_eig  @b7e80d2e
  why    Every guess that occupies orbitals diagonalizes some matrix in the
         orthonormal basis with the eigensolver the run picked; this is that
         one call.
  A      the matrix to diagonalize, on the basis functions, `[n, n]`
  X      the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`
  m      how many of X's columns are kept
  opts   the options column array; `opts[OPT_EIGEN]` picks the eigensolver

gs_spins  @e62b11b2
  why    A guess ends by filling the lowest orbitals with the alpha and beta
         electrons, one electron per orbital per spin.
  X      the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`
  V      the eigenvectors in the orthonormal basis, lowest first, `[m, m]`
  m      the size of the orthonormal basis
  na     the number of alpha (spin-up) electrons
  nb     the number of beta (spin-down) electrons

gs_occupy  @6b8d68d9
  why    The core and GWH guesses differ only in the matrix they diagonalize;
         this is everything after that choice.
  A      the matrix whose eigenvectors become the orbitals, `[n, n]`
  S      the overlap matrix of the basis functions, `[n, n]`
  opts   the options column array: electron counts, orthogonalizer, eigensolver
  X      the orthogonalizer the run picked, `[n, m]`
  m      how many independent combinations of basis functions X keeps
  R      the eigensolver's result: eigenvalues and eigenvectors, `[m + 1, m]`

gs_fractional  @b38a6316
  why    In the spin-averaged SCF all electrons share one set of orbitals.
         When the highest occupied level is degenerate and only partly
         filled, such as a pair of pi orbitals holding three electrons,
         filling one of them and not the other would break a symmetry the
         molecule has; sharing the electrons equally keeps the degenerate
         orbitals equivalent
         [test "fractional occupations share the partly filled level equally"].
  vals   the orbital energies, ascending, `[m]`
  N      how many electrons, both spins together
  m      how many orbitals
  f      the occupation of each orbital, `[m]`
  top    the orbital the last electron goes into
  lo     the lowest orbital of the partly filled level
  hi     the highest orbital of the partly filled level

  = lo#2     widen the level down over orbitals within `GS_AVG_DEGEN` of the top one
  = hi#2     and up

gs_frac_density  @2f121245
  why    The spin-averaged SCF's density has fractional occupations, so each
         orbital is weighted by its own occupation: `C diag(f) C^T`.
  X      the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`
  V      the eigenvectors in the orthonormal basis, `[m, m]`
  m      the size of the orthonormal basis
  f      the occupation of each orbital
  nocc   how many orbitals have electrons
  C      the occupied orbitals on the basis functions, `[n, nocc]`
  Cf     C with each column times its occupation
  k      an orbital

gs_avg  @8c4b7a62
  why    A guess that fills orbitals one spin at a time can commit an
         unpaired electron to the wrong one of two degenerate orbitals, and the
         SCF then converges to an excited state. Solving the SCF first with the
         electrons spread evenly over the partly filled level avoids that
         choice. From this guess UHF on OH reaches PySCF's ground state; from
         the core guess it reaches the same state with DIIS leaving out the
         guess's Fock matrix (`diis_start = 1`, the default) and an excited
         one with the guess's matrix in the history; lib/hf/uhf's tests check
         all three.
  how    Start from the GWH matrix, occupy with fractional numbers, and
         iterate a closed-shell SCF with DIIS until the commutator is below
         `GS_AVG_GTOL` or `GS_AVG_MAXIT` steps have run. Then fill the lowest
         `na` orbitals for alpha and the lowest `nb` for beta.
  H      the one-electron Hamiltonian, kinetic energy plus nuclear
         attraction, `[n, n]`
  S      the overlap matrix, `[n, n]`
  Gp     the packed electron repulsion integrals, which the averaged SCF needs
         to build J and K
  D0     not read
  opts   the options column array of the run
  X      the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`
  m      the size of the orthonormal basis
  N      the number of electrons, both spins
  o      a copy of opts with DIIS on and no level shift, whatever the run asked for
  diis   the DIIS history of the averaged SCF
  U      `S X` over the kept columns, which moves each density into the orthonormal basis
  R      the latest eigenvalues and eigenvectors
  V      the latest eigenvectors, `[m, m]`
  f      the fractional occupations
  nocc   how many orbitals hold electrons
  D      the averaged total density, `[n, n]`
  it     iterations run
  err    the largest entry of the commutator: the averaged SCF's error
  JK     J and K of the averaged density
  F      its Fock matrix `H + J - K/2`
  Fp     that Fock matrix in the orthonormal basis
  E      the commutator of Fp and the density in the orthonormal basis
  Fx     the Fock matrix DIIS extrapolated

  = o        the averaged SCF always uses DIIS and no level shift, so the guess does not change with the run's accelerator
  = D        the starting density: GWH orbitals with fractional occupations
  = Fx       mix the recent Fock matrices by DIIS
  = D#2      the next averaged density

gs_core  @55bdeb8f
  why    The simplest guess: ignore the repulsion between electrons and fill
         the orbitals of the core Hamiltonian. It needs only H and S, and it is
         PySCF's `1e` guess, so it reproduces PySCF's starting density
         [test "the core guess on water 6-31G has PySCF's norm under every orthogonalizer and eigensolver"].
  H      the one-electron Hamiltonian
  S      the overlap matrix
  Gp     not read
  D0     not read
  opts   the options column array

gs_gwh_matrix  @bfe512ac
  why    Diagonal elements of H are atomic-orbital energies; the GWH rule
         estimates the coupling of two functions as their overlap times the
         mean of those energies, scaled by `GS_GWH_K`. That builds in some of
         the bonding the core Hamiltonian's eigenvectors miss.
  H      the one-electron Hamiltonian, `[n, n]`
  S      the overlap matrix, `[n, n]`
  n      the number of basis functions
  A      the GWH matrix, `[n, n]`
  i      a row of A
  j      a column of A

gs_gwh  @f102bdcd
  why    A second cheap guess, different from the core one
         [test "the core and GWH guesses differ, and every guess works on a basis with a dropped vector"].
  H      the one-electron Hamiltonian
  S      the overlap matrix
  Gp     not read
  D0     not read
  opts   the options column array

gs_zero  @7a127848
  why    With zero densities the first Fock matrix is H, so the first
         iteration does what the core guess does; it tests that the loop
         starts correctly from empty densities.
  H      the one-electron Hamiltonian, read for its size
  S      not read
  Gp     not read
  D0     not read
  opts   not read
  n      the number of basis functions

gs_import  @75673c05
  why    How a density from elsewhere, such as PySCF's, enters an SCF run.
         It returns a copy, so the run cannot change the caller's array.
  H      not read
  S      not read
  Gp     not read
  D0     the caller's spin densities, `[2, n, n]`
  opts   not read

gs_methods  @934a4c8c
  why    The SCF picks its guess by number (`OPT_GUESS`), and tests loop over
         every member.

gs_of  @f391cb3c
  why    The SCF calls one def whatever guess the options pick, and this def
         calls each member by name, so the default options reach `gs_avg`
         without a registry.
  H      the one-electron Hamiltonian
  S      the overlap matrix
  Gp     the packed integrals, for `gs_avg`
  D0     the densities to import, for `gs_import`
  opts   the options column array; `opts[OPT_GUESS]` picks the member
  k      the member the options name: `GS_AVG`, `GS_CORE`, `GS_GWH`, `GS_ZERO` or `GS_IMPORT`
